To check your system drive for errors on Windows, you can use the built-in "CHKDSK" (Check Disk) command. This command scans the file system and repairs any errors found on the specified drive. Here's how to check your system drive for errors:

1. Open the Command Prompt (Windows Terminal in Windows 11).

	* In Windows 11, you can search for "Command Prompt" in the Start menu or press the Windows key + X and select "Windows Terminal."
	* In Windows 10, you can search for "Command Prompt" in the Start menu or press the Windows key + X and select "Command Prompt."

1. Type the following command to check your system drive (usually the C: drive) for errors:
```
chkdsk C: /f
```
1. Press Enter.

The "chkdsk" command will scan the C: drive and attempt to fix any errors it finds. If the drive is currently in use, the command will schedule a scan the next time the drive is rebooted.

1. To force the scan to run at the next reboot, type the following command and press Enter:
```
chkdsk C: /r
```
1. Restart your computer to run the scan.

After the system reboots, the "chkdsk" command will scan the C: drive and fix any errors it finds. Once the scan is complete, your system drive should be free of any detected errors.
